From: Foyer Studio <noreply@foyer-studio.dev>
Subject: [PATCH] dummy backend: absolute-time sleep on Linux

Lock the audio clock to wall clock when the audio thread runs SCHED_OTHER
(containers without CAP_SYS_NICE — Cloud Run gen2, plain `docker run`).

The original timing loop calls `Glib::usleep(N)` which sleeps AT LEAST
N µs; on a non-RT thread under CPU contention the actual sleep can be
5–20 ms longer than asked. Each cycle's sleep target is computed from
when the previous cycle ENDED (not from a fixed grid), so over-sleep
accumulates. Result: the engine's wall-clock cycle rate falls below
`samplerate / period`, the sample stream is produced ~7-10 % slower
than realtime, and any downstream consumer that plays at fixed sample
rate (e.g. a browser AudioContext at 48 kHz, or hardware via PulseAudio
resampling) underruns continuously, producing audible pops on every
period boundary that runs dry.

Fix: maintain `_target_cycle_us` as a monotonically-advancing absolute
deadline (one nominal_time per cycle), and use
`clock_nanosleep(CLOCK_MONOTONIC, TIMER_ABSTIME)` to wake at that
absolute time. Scheduling delay in cycle N doesn't push cycle N+1's
deadline forward; the kernel just wakes us closer to (or right at) the
target. If we fall more than a handful of cycles behind we reset the
deadline rather than burst-process to catch up (which would itself be
audible).

Linux-only — other platforms keep the original drift-accumulating
behavior.

---
diff --git a/libs/backends/dummy/dummy_audiobackend.cc b/libs/backends/dummy/dummy_audiobackend.cc
index d753681870..4271ed4980 100644
--- a/libs/backends/dummy/dummy_audiobackend.cc
+++ b/libs/backends/dummy/dummy_audiobackend.cc
@@ -22,6 +22,10 @@
 #include <math.h>
 #include <sys/time.h>
 #include <stdlib.h>
+#ifdef __linux__
+#include <time.h>   /* Foyer Studio: clock_nanosleep + struct timespec */
+#include <errno.h>  /* Foyer Studio: EINTR retry on clock_nanosleep */
+#endif
 
 #include <glibmm.h>
 
@@ -966,14 +970,60 @@ DummyAudioBackend::main_process_thread ()
 			_dsp_load_calc.set_stop_timestamp_us (_x_get_monotonic_usec());
 			_dsp_load = _dsp_load_calc.get_dsp_load_unbound ();
 
-			const int64_t elapsed_time = _dsp_load_calc.elapsed_time_us ();
 			const int64_t nominal_time = _dsp_load_calc.get_max_time_us ();
+#ifdef __linux__
+			/* Foyer Studio: absolute-time sleep on Linux to keep the audio
+			 * clock locked to wall clock on non-RT threads (containers
+			 * without CAP_SYS_NICE — Cloud Run gen2, plain `docker run`).
+			 *
+			 * The original implementation calls `Glib::usleep(N)` which
+			 * sleeps AT LEAST N µs; on a SCHED_OTHER thread under CPU
+			 * contention the actual sleep can be 5–20 ms longer than
+			 * asked. Since each cycle's sleep target is computed from
+			 * the time the previous cycle ENDED (not from a fixed grid),
+			 * over-sleep accumulates. Result: the engine's wall-clock
+			 * cycle rate falls below `samplerate / period`, the sample
+			 * stream is produced ~7-10 % slower than realtime, and any
+			 * downstream consumer that plays at fixed sample rate (e.g.
+			 * a browser AudioContext at 48 kHz, or hardware via PulseAudio
+			 * resampling) underruns continuously, producing audible
+			 * pops on every period boundary that runs dry.
+			 *
+			 * Fix: maintain `_target_cycle_us` as a monotonically-advancing
+			 * absolute deadline (one nominal_time per cycle), and use
+			 * `clock_nanosleep(CLOCK_MONOTONIC, TIMER_ABSTIME)` to wake
+			 * at that absolute time. Scheduling delay in cycle N
+			 * doesn't push cycle N+1's deadline forward; the kernel
+			 * just wakes us closer to (or right at) the target. If we
+			 * fall more than a handful of cycles behind we reset the
+			 * deadline rather than burst-process to catch up (which
+			 * would itself be audible).
+			 */
+			if (_target_cycle_us == 0) {
+				_target_cycle_us = _x_get_monotonic_usec();
+			}
+			_target_cycle_us += static_cast<int64_t> (_speedup * nominal_time);
+			const int64_t now_us = _x_get_monotonic_usec();
+			if (_target_cycle_us > now_us + 10) {
+				struct timespec ts;
+				ts.tv_sec  =  _target_cycle_us / 1000000;
+				ts.tv_nsec = (_target_cycle_us % 1000000) * 1000;
+				while (clock_nanosleep (CLOCK_MONOTONIC, TIMER_ABSTIME, &ts, NULL) == EINTR) {}
+			} else if (now_us - _target_cycle_us > nominal_time * 5) {
+				/* > 5 cycles behind — give up catching up, re-anchor. */
+				_target_cycle_us = now_us;
+				Glib::usleep (10);
+			}
+			/* else: slightly behind, run next cycle immediately. */
+#else
+			const int64_t elapsed_time = _dsp_load_calc.elapsed_time_us ();
 			if (elapsed_time < nominal_time) {
 				const int64_t sleepy = _speedup * (nominal_time - elapsed_time);
 				Glib::usleep (std::max ((int64_t) 10, sleepy));
 			} else {
 				Glib::usleep (10); // don't hog cpu
 			}
+#endif
 		} else {
 			_dsp_load = 1.0f;
 			Glib::usleep (10); // don't hog cpu
diff --git a/libs/backends/dummy/dummy_audiobackend.h b/libs/backends/dummy/dummy_audiobackend.h
index 610f05ef3d..89a50d873d 100644
--- a/libs/backends/dummy/dummy_audiobackend.h
+++ b/libs/backends/dummy/dummy_audiobackend.h
@@ -384,6 +384,15 @@ class DummyAudioBackend : public AudioBackend, public PortEngineSharedImpl
 
 		samplecnt_t _processed_samples;
 
+		// Foyer Studio: absolute-time cycle deadline (µs since some
+		// arbitrary monotonic epoch). Advances by nominal_time per
+		// cycle; the audio thread sleeps to this deadline using
+		// clock_nanosleep(TIMER_ABSTIME) to lock the audio clock to
+		// wall clock without needing RT scheduling. See the
+		// implementation note in main_process_thread() for the full
+		// rationale.
+		int64_t _target_cycle_us = 0;
+
 		pthread_t _main_thread;
 
 		/* process threads */
